===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|recompensen}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|recompenser}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|recompenso|recompensare}}, from {{der|en|la|re-||again}} + {{m|la|compenso|compensare|to balance out}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[reward]] or [[repay]] (someone) for something done, given etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1596|author=w:Edmund Spenser|title=w:The Faerie Queene|section=IV.ii|text=She in regard thereof him '''recompenst''' / With golden words, and goodly countenance, / And such fond [[favor|fauour]]s sparingly dispenst {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|II|iii|passage=He cannot '''recompense''' me better.}}
# To give [[compensation]] for an injury, or other type of harm or damage.
#: {{ux|en|The judge ordered the [[defendant]] to '''recompense''' the [[plaintiff]] by paying $100.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To give (something) in return; to [[pay back]]; to pay, as something earned or deserved.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Romans|12|17|passage='''Recompense''' to no man evil for evil.}}

